        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "Alcohols react with sodium metal, releasing hydrogen: 2C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + 2Na → 2C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>ONa + H<sub>2</sub>. Both SOCl<sub>2</sub> and PCl<sub>5</sub> replace the OH group with Cl to give chloroethane: C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + SOCl<sub>2</sub> → C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Cl + SO<sub>2</sub> + HCl and C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + PCl<sub>5</sub> → C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Cl + POCl<sub>3</sub> + HCl.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:16.1(2)"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "optionAnalysis": [
            {
              "key": "A",
              "correct": true,
              "text": "All three react: Na gives sodium ethoxide and H<sub>2</sub>; SOCl<sub>2</sub> and PCl<sub>5</sub> both convert ethanol to chloroethane."
            },
            {
              "key": "B",
              "correct": false,
              "text": "Leaves out SOCl<sub>2</sub> and PCl<sub>5</sub>, which are both standard reagents for turning an alcohol into a chloroalkane."
            },
            {
              "key": "C",
              "correct": false,
              "text": "Leaves out sodium; alcohols react with Na(s) to give an alkoxide and hydrogen gas."
            },
            {
              "key": "D",
              "correct": false,
              "text": "Leaves out both Na and SOCl<sub>2</sub>."
            }
          ],
          "reasoningChain": [
